#5
Assume that x11 is initialized to 11 and x12 is initialized to 22. Suppose you executed 
the code below on a version of the pipeline that does not handle data hazards. What would 
the final values of register x15 be? Assume the register file is written at the beginning 
of the cycle and read at the end of a cycle. Therefore, an ID stage will return the 
results of a WB state occurring during the same cycle.

addi x11, x12, 5
add  x13, x11, x12
addi x14, x11, 15
add  x15, x11, x11


Init states:
x11 is initialized to 11.
x12 is initialized to 22.

    addi x11, x12, 5

This instruction adds the imm 5 to the content of x12 and stores the result in x11 => x11 = 27.

    add x13, x11, x12
  
This instruction adds the value of x11 (27, given that the pipeline doesn't handle data hazards 
but performs writes at the beginning and reads at the end of the cycle, allowing for the update 
to x11 in the previous instruction to be visible here) to the value of x12 (22), storing the 
result in x13 => x13 = 49.

    addi x14, x11, 15

This instruction adds the imm15 to the content of x11 (27) and stores the result in x14 => x14 = 42.

    add x15, x11, x11

This instruction adds the content of x11 to itself. x11 = 27 => x15 = 54.


Considering the pipeline's behavior, the ordering guarantees that register modifications 
inside the loop are observable by following instructions.

The given description aligns with a sequential execution model, where instructions perceive 
the outcomes of preceding instructions as they execute, reinforcing the anticipated results 
based on sequential execution rationale.

After executing the provided instructions, x15 will hold the value 54.

#6
Add NOP instructions to the code below so that it will run correctly on a pipeline that does not handle data hazards.

Given code:

    addi x11, x12, 5
    add x13, x11, x12
    addi x14, x11, 15
    add x15, x13, x12

To ensure correct execution on a pipeline without data hazard handling, we need to insert NOP 
instructions to create delays, allowing each instruction to complete its write-back before 
subsequent dependent instructions begin. This approach helps prevent read-after-write (RAW) hazards.

result:
1    addi x11, x12, 5
2    NOP
3    NOP
4    add  x13, x11, x12
5    addi x14, x11, 15
6    NOP
7    NOP
8    add  x15, x13, x12

Description:
1) writes to x11
2) delay slot
3) delay slot => write to x11 is completed before it's read operation
4) read x11 - Safe to execute. Previous write operation has finished.
5) Proceeding without a NOP for x14 following an operation involving x11 is safe as there are no immediate dependencies on x14 in the subsequent instructions.
6) delay slot before using x13 to ensure its value is updated.
7) delay slot fully committed write operation
8) It is safe to proceed with the execution.

This order of operations grants each instruction sufficient time to finalize the recording of its outcomes in the register file 
prior to the commencement of subsequent instructions, thereby diminishing data hazards in a pipeline that lacks mechanisms for handling such issues.
